<div class=3D"wrap">

  <header>
    <span class=3D"eyebrow">Genius Hacks Q3 2026 =C2=B7 Risk Assessment Wor=
kbench</span>
    <h1>One Case, Two Timelines</h1>
    <p class=3D"sub">The same real-world request, walked through today's em=
ail-and-spreadsheet process and through the agent-built Workbench =E2=80=94=
 written for anyone, no banking background required.</p>
  </header>

  <div class=3D"prose">
    <p><strong>First, what is this "FCRM" thing, and why does a bank need i=
t?</strong> Banks are legally required to make sure they aren't accidentall=
y helping criminals move money, evade sanctions, or fund illegal activity. =
So any time a bank wants to try something new =E2=80=94 sell a new product,=
 work with a new type of customer, open in a new country =E2=80=94 a specia=
lized team has to stop and ask: <em>could this new thing make it easier for=
 bad actors to abuse us, and are we set up to handle it responsibly?</em> T=
hat team is called Financial Crimes Risk Management, or FCRM. Think of them=
 as the bank's safety inspectors for money crime: nothing new launches unti=
l they've signed off.</p>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"callout">
    <h3>The scenario: banking cannabis dispensaries</h3>
    <p>Say the bank's Commercial Banking team wants to start opening busine=
ss accounts for licensed cannabis dispensaries in Colorado and Michigan, wh=
ere selling cannabis is legal under state law. Here's the catch: cannabis i=
s still illegal under <em>federal</em> law, and this bank is federally regu=
lated. Dispensaries also tend to handle unusually large amounts of cash, be=
cause many card companies won't work with them =E2=80=94 and cash-heavy bus=
inesses are exactly the kind criminals like to use to hide illegally-earned=
 money. So before Commercial Banking can say yes to a single dispensary cus=
tomer, FCRM has to work out: is this legal for us, how risky is it, and if =
we do it, under what conditions? The decision can land on four outcomes =E2=
=80=94 approved, rejected, deferred, or approved with conditions. This walk=
through follows the case to the outcome it actually reaches:</p>
    <span class=3D"outcome-tag">APPROVED =C2=B7 WITH CONDITIONS</span>
  </div>

